faq:
  - q: "Is cured in place pipe a repair or a replacement?"
    a: "Functionally it is a replacement: a new, jointless, structurally designed pipe is created inside the old one, and specifications size it as if the old pipe were gone. Procedurally it is a repair, because nothing is excavated or removed. That is why both terms get used, and why insurance and permitting sometimes treat it as one or the other."
  - q: "Can a sewer belly be fixed with a liner?"
    a: "No. A belly is a low spot where the pipe has settled and holds standing water. A liner follows the pipe exactly, so it reproduces the belly. The sagging section has to be excavated and re-laid on proper grade, after which the rest of the run can be lined."
  - q: "What is a CIPP point repair?"
    a: "A short liner, often two to four feet, installed over a single defect such as a cracked joint or a root intrusion, instead of lining the whole pipe. It is placed with a packer, cured in place and leaves the rest of the pipe untouched. It makes sense when the pipe is otherwise sound."
  - q: "Can you line a collapsed pipe?"
    a: "Not through the collapse. If the pipe has lost its shape or a section is missing, the liner has nothing to form against and the inversion head or cutter cannot pass. The collapsed section is excavated and replaced, or the line is pipe-burst, and lining can then be used on the remaining length."
  - q: "How do I read the camera report?"
    a: "Look for the pipe material and diameter, the distance marker at each defect, and the words the operator uses. Cracks, fractures, root intrusion, offset joints and corrosion are lining candidates. Collapse, missing wall, severe deformation and sag with standing water are not. Ask the operator to point out which defects fall in which category."
  - q: "Does lining fix root problems for good?"
    a: "Roots enter at joints and cracks. A liner has neither, so once it is installed the roots have no way in. Roots already in the pipe are cut out during cleaning before the liner goes in. The trees stay; the pathway is gone."
