<p class="cl-lead">A camera goes down the sewer and comes back with a list of problems. Now someone has to decide: line it, patch one spot, or dig it up. Each is the right answer for a particular kind of damage, and the wrong answer for the others. This page is about telling them apart, so that when a contractor recommends a cured in place pipe repair, or says it will not work, you know why.</p>

<h2>Three ways to fix a failed sewer</h2>
<table class="cl-table">
  <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it does</th><th>Best for</th><th>Not for</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Full-length lining</strong></td><td>A new structural pipe is cured inside the old one from one access point to the next.</td><td>Cracks, fractures, root intrusion at many joints, corrosion and scale, leaking joints, Orangeburg that still holds shape.</td><td>Collapse, missing sections, bellies, pipe too small to lose any diameter.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Point repair</strong></td><td>A short liner is cured over one defect; the rest of the pipe is left as is.</td><td>A single bad joint, one root entry, one crack in an otherwise sound pipe.</td><td>Pipe with defects every few feet (line the whole thing instead), bellies, collapse.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Excavate or pipe-burst</strong></td><td>The bad pipe is dug out and replaced, or a bursting head breaks it outward while pulling a new pipe through.</td><td>Collapse, bellies, missing wall, undersized pipe that needs upsizing, Orangeburg that has flattened.</td><td>Pipe under a slab, driveway, mature trees or a public street when lining would do the job.</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>Defects a liner fixes</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Cracks and fractures</strong>, longitudinal or circumferential, as long as the pipe still holds its round shape. The liner is designed to carry the soil and groundwater load on its own, so a cracked host pipe is exactly what the engineering assumes.</li>
  <li><strong>Root intrusion.</strong> Roots are cut out during cleaning. The liner seals the joint or crack they came through. This is the single most common reason residential pipes get lined.</li>
  <li><strong>Offset joints</strong> up to a point. A small offset is ground smooth by a robotic cutter and lined over; a large offset where the pipe sections no longer overlap is a point repair.</li>
  <li><strong>Corrosion and scale</strong> in cast iron. The pipe is descaled, then lined; the liner is immune to the chemistry that ate the iron.</li>
  <li><strong>Leaking joints and infiltration.</strong> A jointless liner stops groundwater coming in and sewage going out.</li>
  <li><strong>Orangeburg</strong> (bituminous fiber pipe) that is still round. Once it starts to oval or blister, it is a candidate for bursting instead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Defects a liner cannot fix</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Collapse or missing wall.</strong> There is nothing for the liner to form against, and the equipment cannot pass. Excavate that section first; the rest of the run can often still be lined.</li>
  <li><strong>Bellies and reverse grade.</strong> The liner follows the pipe, including the sag. Standing water in the camera recording at a fixed point is the giveaway.</li>
  <li><strong>Severe deformation.</strong> A pipe crushed to an oval may not take a liner designed for a round bore; a lining contractor will tell you the deformation limit of their system.</li>
  <li><strong>Undersized pipe.</strong> If the line is already too small for the fixtures on it, losing a few millimeters of diameter makes it worse. Pipe bursting can upsize; lining cannot.</li>
  <li><strong>Pipes full of connections in a short span.</strong> Each one must be reinstated, and at some density excavation becomes simpler.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Reading the camera report</h2>
<p>A good inspection report gives you the pipe material, the diameter, a footage marker for every defect and a plain description of each. Line them up against the two lists above. Then ask two questions. First, <em>is any single defect disqualifying?</em> One collapse or one belly means at least a point excavation before anything else. Second, <em>how many defects are there and how far apart?</em> One or two in a long, otherwise sound pipe points to a point repair; defects every few feet point to full-length lining. The <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/installation">cured in place pipe installation</a> page explains what the crew does with that report on lining day.</p>

<h2>The hybrid job</h2>
<p>Many real repairs combine methods. A common one: a belly or a collapsed joint near the property line is excavated and replaced with a few feet of new pipe, and the remaining run under the house and yard is lined in the same visit. The owner pays for one small hole instead of a trench the length of the lot. Several of the projects posted by the installers on this site are exactly this shape; the <a href="/cured-in-place-pipe/cost">cured in place pipe cost</a> page shows how that pencils out against a full replacement bid.</p>

